    "reference": "Judges XIII, XIV, XV, XVI.",
    "narrative": "1. Samson possessed extraordinary strength, 2. and it lay in his hair. 3. This Samson went courting among the Philistines, 4. and that against his parents' will. 5. On the way a lion met him, 6. which he tore apart with his hands, as one tears apart a young goat. 7. When he traveled the road again, the bees had made a honeycomb in the lion's carcass. 8. He took a piece of the honey with him, but did not say where he had found it. 9. At the wedding Samson proposed a riddle to the guests, which they were to guess within seven days. 10. If they guessed the riddle, he promised them thirty shirts and thirty festive garments. 11. But if they could not guess it, the Philistines were to give him thirty festive garments. 12. The riddle was this: Food came out of the eater, and sweetness out of the strong. 13. When the Philistines could not solve the riddle, 14. they brought the bride over to their side, 15. and she gave her bridegroom no peace until she had learned the secret from him and betrayed it to the Philistines. 16. When the seven days were over, the Philistines said: What is sweeter than honey, and what is stronger than a lion? 17. Samson answered them: If you had not plowed with my heifer, you would not have guessed my riddle. 18. Thereupon he went and killed thirty Philistines and gave their garments to those who had guessed the riddle. 19. When Samson then wished to have his bride, her father had given her to another. 20. So Samson went and caught three hundred foxes. 21. He tied their tails together, two by two; 22. between the tails he tied a firebrand, 23. and let them run among the Philistines' grain. 24. The Philistines became angry at this and burned Samson's bride together with her father's entire household. 25. After this Samson was bound with ropes and delivered to his enemies. 26. But Samson broke the ropes, 27. and killed a thousand Philistines with an ass's jawbone. 28. Another time the Philistines surrounded Samson in the city of Gaza while he was with a prostitute. 29. But Samson rose at midnight, lifted out the city gate, and carried it to the top of a mountain. 30. Finally Samson fell in love with a woman named Delilah. 31. She was bribed by the Philistines to reveal to them where his strength lay. 32. Samson indeed deceived this Delilah three times in succession with false information. 33. For the first time he allowed himself to be bound with seven cords of bast. 34. When this had been done, Delilah said to him: The Philistines are upon you, Samson! 35. He then broke the cords like a flaxen string. 36. The second time he allowed himself to be bound with new ropes, 37. and Delilah cried again: The Philistines are upon you, Samson! 38. But Samson broke the ropes like threads of twine. 39. The third time he allowed the locks of his hair to be nailed down. 40. But when Delilah cried once more: The Philistines are upon you, Samson! 41. he tore the locks of his hair together with the nails out of the wall. 42. At last, however, with her smooth words she extracted the secret that his strength was hidden in his hair. 43. Immediately she summoned the Philistines to lie in wait for him. 44. While he was asleep in her lap, she had the locks of his hair shorn off without his noticing. 45. As soon as this had happened, Samson realized that the Lord had departed from him. 46. The Philistines thus gained power over him and put out his eyes. 47. They then bound him with two brazen chains and compelled him to grind in the mill. 48. Meanwhile, as his hair grew again, his strength also returned. 49. When the Philistines held a great sacrifice, they had Samson brought in so that he might entertain them. 50. The house in which they were assembled stood upon two pillars. 51. There were three thousand spectators. 52. Samson grasped both pillars with his hands and brought the whole house down in a heap. 53. Thus Samson and his enemies were slain together.",
