    "reference": "Daniel VI.",
    "narrative": "1. King Darius of Babylon wished to set the prophet Daniel over the whole kingdom on account of his great understanding. 2. This vexed the others at court, and they sought to overthrow Daniel. 3. They therefore prevailed upon King Darius to issue a decree which Daniel could not observe. 4. Namely, it was commanded that for thirty days no one should ask anything except of the king alone. 5. And if anyone should call upon another God, he was immediately to be thrown into the lions' den. 6. Daniel did not observe this decree, but knelt down three times every day and worshiped the true God. 7. When King Darius heard this, he was greatly concerned for Daniel and wished to deliver him. 8. But the people would not listen to reason, and Daniel had to go down into the lions' den. 9. King Darius, however, said to him: May thy God, whom thou servest without ceasing, help thee! 10. He also sealed the opening of the den with his seal, so that no one might do Daniel any harm. 11. Indeed, the king could not eat in the evening for anxiety, nor could he sleep at night. 12. Early in the morning the king came to the lions' den and cried: Daniel, thou servant of the living God, hath thy God been able to deliver thee from the lions? 13. Daniel answered: My God hath sent his angel, who hath shut the mouths of the lions, so that they have done me no harm. 14. Darius rejoiced at this and had Daniel drawn out of the den. 15. But the men who had accused him he had cast into the den with their wives and children. 16. And before they even reached the ground, they were crushed by the lions. 17. Thereupon King Darius issued a decree that everyone should fear and stand in awe of the God of Daniel. 18. When King Cyrus reigned after this, the Babylonians had an idol called Bel. 19. To it twelve measures of wheat, forty sheep, and three buckets of wine had to be sacrificed daily. 20. The priests consumed these things with their households, 21. and made the king believe that the idol needed so much every day. 22. Daniel revealed this deceit to the king, and King Cyrus wished to discover the truth. 23. He therefore set the sacrifice before Bel himself and sealed the door of the temple with his royal ring. 24. When the king came into the temple again, the seal was intact and the sacrifice was entirely consumed. 25. Thereupon the king cried: Bel, thou art a great God, and there is no deceit with thee. 26. But Daniel showed the king all manner of footprints of men, women, and children on the floor of the temple. 27. For during the night the priests and their households had eaten, drunk, and reveled in the temple. 28. Daniel, however, had had the floor of the temple strewn with ashes the evening before. 29. Then the king had the priests seized and compelled them to show him the secret passage under the table. 30. When the deceit was discovered, the king had the priests put to death, and Daniel was allowed to destroy Bel. 31. There was also a great dragon in Babylon which was worshiped as a God. 32. But Daniel made little cakes of pitch, fat, and hair and cast them into the dragon's mouth. 33. Now when the dragon burst asunder from this, Daniel said: Behold, these are your gods. 34. Then Daniel was again cast by the people into the lions' den and had to sit among them for six days. 35. There were seven lions in the den, 36. and they were given two men and two sheep daily. 37. But for those six days they were given nothing, so that they might the sooner devour Daniel. 38. At that time the prophet Habakkuk wished to bring food to his reapers in the field; 39. an angel of the Lord took him by the head and carried him to Babylon into the lions' den. 40. When Daniel had eaten, the angel brought the prophet Habakkuk back to his place. 41. On the seventh day the king came to the den and let Daniel out again. 42. And in his stead he had his enemies thrown into the den, 43. where they were immediately devoured by the lions before the eyes of the king.",
